WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.120
Making a solo video that looks like a high-budget film comes down to three things.

2
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.760
Framing, lighting and movement.

3
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:13.760
Creating a cinematic video completely solo is honestly just like building a Lego set.

4
00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.880
Framing is your baseplate that holds everything together.

5
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.600
The minifigure in the middle is you,

6
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.560
and the lighting acts like those, you know, bright-colored bricks

7
00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.040
that bring the building to life and gives a dimension.

8
00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:26.240
And the movement.

9
00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.320
That's turning the model around so people can see it from every angle.

10
00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:33.040
Miss one of those and the whole thing falls apart.

11
00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:36.240
For me, every video starts with an exciting idea,

12
00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.480
a cool camera angle I won't test out, or sometimes it's just a song.

13
00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.000
And that soundtrack part actually matters more than people realize,

14
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.320
because when you don't have a crew, the music actually acts as your director.

15
00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:52.800
I usually hear a track that hits and instantly imagine the whole video

16
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.720
before I even start writing the script.

17
00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.080
You definitely should not leave the music for post-production.

18
00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:01.360
Picking the music early dictates the pacing, energy

19
00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:04.240
and the general vibe of the video before you even start shooting.

20
00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:08.240
Once I have the vision, I turn it into a script and shot list.

21
00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:11.760
If you can draw, you can sketch out the scene so you don't forget your idea.

22
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:15.120
Or usually I just research on social media or Pinterest

23
00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:17.920
and find images that match the vision in my head.

24
00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:21.280
So how do you actually turn a small bedroom into a movie set?

25
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.880
The hardest part about filming solo is running back and forth

26
00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:27.200
to check if you're in focus or frame rate.

27
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:30.240
Here's the cheat code, connect your phone to your camera.

28
00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.720
I personally use this app called Sony Creators

29
00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:36.960
that connects my camera to my phone and turns it into a live monitor.

30
00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.240
So I just sit down, I turn it on the app

31
00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:45.120
and I lock my manual focus and check my exposure right from the spot.

32
00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:49.200
Once the monitor is locked in, let's talk about camera angles and lenses.

33
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:53.680
I start with my main direct shot, keeping the lens wide around 24mm

34
00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:56.160
so it actually captures the whole vibe of the room.

35
00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.800
For this, I use my Sony FX3.

36
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.280
Yes, it's definitely on the pricey side.

37
00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.520
But as my channel started growing,

38
00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.400
taking that step to upgrading my visual quality

39
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.800
was a bold move I really wanted to make.

40
00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.120
And for my wide direct shots, I sometimes also use my DJI Osmo.

41
00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.960
It's super small and the best thing I like about it is the tracking feature.

42
00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:19.040
But whatever camera you're using,

43
00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:22.400
do not leave your tripod flat at eye level.

44
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.320
It just looks like a boring zoom call.

45
00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:27.840
Drop the camera to chest height and tilt it slightly upward.

46
00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.440
It instantly makes your room feel bigger and more cinematic.

47
00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.800
And here's a pro tip that people overlook all the time.

48
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.800
That's your set composition.

49
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:39.840
The overall visual environment matters insanely.

50
00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:42.480
Your frame should never feel empty or dead.

51
00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.440
I usually put a desk lamp or bring in a plant

52
00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:48.160
or place a stack of books in the corner of my table

53
00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:51.680
because simply dressing your set completely changes the texture

54
00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:53.040
and the mood of the shot.

55
00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.960
And from there, I set up a secondary camera angle from the side.

56
00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:02.000
I swing my tripod, switch to a tighter 50mm or 85mm lens

57
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.560
and push the camera all the way back against the wall.

58
00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.040
Tighter lenses physically compress space,

59
00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:09.840
meaning your background is visually pulled closer to you

60
00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.080
and thrown into a rich background blur.

61
00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:16.160
I locked my Sony ZV-E10 Mark II for this profile shot.

62
00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:18.240
This is actually my OG camera,

63
00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.480
the one I started my creative journey with

64
00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:23.680
and it's still an absolute beast for solo creators

65
00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.880
because it shoots full 10-bit slog shots.

66
00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:30.560
Having it off to the side gives the scene that behind-the-scenes vibe.

67
00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.280
The thing that makes your videos instantly cinematic

68
00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:34.800
are your micro details.

69
00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.040
You can park a tiny camera right at your desk

70
00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:40.240
just inches away from your hands at a slight angle

71
00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:43.360
and capturing those small physical actions like typing,

72
00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:45.600
turning a button or writing something down

73
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:48.640
as immediate intimacy and texture to your edit.

74
00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:51.680
For B-rolls, you can shoot literally anything.

75
00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.960
It can be you taking a sip, dropping something,

76
00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:56.640
work process details work well

77
00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:59.120
like a tight angle showing a pen tip writing something

78
00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:00.400
or checking off a list.

79
00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:04.640
Another quick angle I love is mounting a camera straight above me, looking down.

80
00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:08.400
This top-down bird's eye view is awesome for showing what you're doing

81
00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:11.280
like you're working, sketching ideas or taking notes.

82
00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:16.480
I know, moving your tripod and setting up multiple cameras by yourself takes time

83
00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:21.040
but trust me, mixing perspectives is primarily what trips the viewer

84
00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:24.320
into thinking there's an entire film crew standing in your room.

85
00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:28.240
Before we actually record anything though, we have to talk about light.

86
00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:30.880
Good lighting will make a cheap phone look like cinema

87
00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:34.560
but bad lighting will make a $5,000 camera look like an iPhone 5.

88
00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:37.440
The secret to a cinematic look isn't expensive gear.

89
00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:39.120
It's contrast and direction.

90
00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.560
You want light shaping in one side of your face and shadow on the other.

91
00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:45.440
Honestly, you don't even need studio ways to start.

92
00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:47.200
Sometimes I just use my window.

93
00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:50.800
The hack here is how you position yourself relatively to it.

94
00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:54.880
If you face the window directly, your face looks completely flat and boring.

95
00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:58.880
But if you turn yourself sideways so the window light hits you at an angle,

96
00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:01.920
you instantly get that natural shadow on the other side.

97
00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:04.560
Setting up your light is really about experimenting.

98
00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.120
When you look at your shot, you should just feel it.

99
00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:11.280
When I'm using my actual studio lights, I replicate the exact same concept.

100
00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.920
I set my main light, my Amaran 300C, in a softbox off to the side

101
00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:18.000
to wrap soft light around my face.

102
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.760
Then I bring in a smaller light, my Amaran 150C,

103
00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:24.480
just to lift the shadows slightly so they aren't pitch black.

104
00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:27.120
And here's a massive budget cheat code.

105
00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:30.000
Grab one of these super cheap pocket MC lights.

106
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.840
I usually tuck a tiny light right behind a plant or a monitor.

107
00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:36.960
I set the color and let it glow, hiding it behind objects.

108
00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:40.960
Creates colorful background accents that gives your room instant depth.

109
00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:45.440
Sometimes I put them in a couple desk lamps in the back to give the room a live mood.

110
00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:48.480
Cinematic lighting isn't about blinding your space.

111
00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:52.720
It's about using whatever light source that you have to paint shape, contrast,

112
00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:54.240
and depth into your frame.

113
00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:57.920
Once the camera and lighting is set, the final piece is movement.

114
00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:00.640
And I'm not just saying you should move your camera around.

115
00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:02.080
I mean moving yourself.

116
00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:06.480
If you just sit frozen in the dead center of the frame, talking for 10 minutes,

117
00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:10.240
your video is going to feel stiff, no matter how good your lighting is.

118
00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:13.440
Think about how characters are framed in anime and movies.

119
00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:14.960
They rarely just stand there.

120
00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:18.400
Sometimes even the most basic and static shot can look cinematic

121
00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:20.320
if you try standing at a different angle.

122
00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:22.560
You can introduce yourself in a creative way,

123
00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:26.320
like walk into the shot carrying coffee, lean over the desk,

124
00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:28.080
or walk around while talking.

125
00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:31.120
This way you can shift different postures, hit different angles,

126
00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:33.840
and let yourself interact naturally with your space.

127
00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:36.960
Filming solo is slow and frustrating.

128
00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:39.440
You'll mess up the focus, you'll realize halfway through

129
00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:42.560
that your lighting is wrong and your batteries ran out,

130
00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:44.080
but there's no way around that.

131
00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:47.600
That's just what it takes to build something entirely on your own.

132
00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:50.800
But you know, over time it actually becomes meditative.

133
00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:54.560
I put on music, I set up my lights, and frame the shot,

134
00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:55.760
and then I get into it.

135
00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:58.800
You don't build a massive LEGO set all at once.

136
00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:02.240
You place the base plate, you click each piece together one by one,

137
00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:04.640
and turn it around to bring the story to life.

138
00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:08.560
Don't wait for the perfect camera, the perfect light, or the room.

139
00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:10.080
Use what you already have.

140
00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:14.000
Build with the pieces you already own and go make your own video.

141
00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.000
Bye everyone, see you in my next video.
